Record history as today, but render only when the screen is usable; hold the rest until unlock.
```kotlin
private val held = java.util.ArrayDeque<Pair<Long, JSONObject>>()
private fun screenUsable() = getSystemService(PowerManager::class.java).isInteractive &&
    !getSystemService(KeyguardManager::class.java).isKeyguardLocked
// "drop" (l.240) :
if (!Prefs.isMuted()) {
    if (screenUsable()) overlay.show(msg)
    else { held.addLast(SystemClock.elapsedRealtime() to msg); while (held.size > 3) held.pollFirst() }
}
// Écran déverrouillé : on montre les drops reçus pendant la veille (≤ 10 min)
private val screenReceiver = object : BroadcastReceiver() {
    override fun onReceive(c: Context, i: Intent) {
        if (!screenUsable()) return
        val now = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime()
        while (true) {
            val (t, m) = held.pollFirst() ?: break
            if (now - t <= 600_000L && !Prefs.isMuted()) overlay.show(m)
        }
    }
}
```
onCreate: register for ACTION_USER_PRESENT + ACTION_SCREEN_ON (API 33+: Context.RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED); onDestroy: runCatching { unregisterReceiver(screenReceiver) }. Simpler alternative if preferred: treat locked/off like quiet hours (history only).

MainActivity: tie the listener to the time the screen is visible, and pause the WebView.
```kotlin
// onCreate : supprimer Hub.addListener(onHubChange)
override fun onStart() {
    super.onStart()
    Hub.addListener(onHubChange)
    web.onResume()
}
override fun onStop() {
    Hub.removeListener(onHubChange)
    web.onPause()
    super.onStop()
}
```
onResume() keeps pushState(), so the screen is refreshed on return; the removeListener in onDestroy can stay (idempotent). app.js:413 (Android-only file): `setInterval(() => { if (!document.hidden) window.onNativeState(); }, 30000);`. Do not use web.pauseTimers(): it applies to the whole process and would freeze the service's overlay WebView.

